   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 426 of the laws of 1983, amending the public health\nlaw relating to professional misconduct proceedings, in relation to the\neffectiveness of certain provisions thereof; to amend chapter 582 of the laws\nof 1984, amending the public health law relating to regulating activities of\nphysicians, in relation to the effectiveness of certain provisions thereof; to\namend chapter 57 of the laws of 2015, amending the social services law and\nother laws relating to energy audits and/or disaster preparedness reviews of\nresidential healthcare facilities by the commissioner, in relation to the\neffectiveness of certain provisions thereof; to amend chapter 81 of the laws\nof 1995, amending the public health law and other laws relating to medical\nreimbursement and welfare reform, in relation to extending the effectiveness\nof certain provisions thereof; and to amend chapter 60 of the laws of 2014,\namending the social services law relating to eliminating prescriber prevails\nfor brand name drugs with generic equivalents, in relation to the\neffectiveness thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S8093",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8093-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §5, Chap 426 of 1983; amd §5, Chap 582 of 1984; amd part B §60, Chap 57 of 2015; amd §246, Chap 81 of 1995; amd part C §71, Chap 60 of 2014",
    "lawSection": "Public Health",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8093\n\nSPONSOR: HANNON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 426 of the laws of 1983, amending the public\nhealth law relating to professional misconduct proceedings, in relation\nto the effectiveness of certain provisions thereof; to amend chapter 582\nof the laws of 1984, amending the public health law relating to regulat-\ning activities of physicians, in relation to the effectiveness of\ncertain provisions thereof; to amend chapter 57 of the laws of 2015,\namending the social services law and other laws relating to energy\naudits and/or disaster preparedness reviews of residential healthcare\nfacilities by the commissioner, in relation to the effectiveness of\ncertain provisions thereof; to amend chapter 81 of the laws of 1995,\namending the public health law and other laws relating to medical\nreimbursement and welfare reform, in relation to extending the effec-\ntiveness of certain provisions thereof; and to amend chapter 60 of the\nlaws of 2014, amending the social services law relating to eliminating\nprescriber prevails for brand name drugs with generic equivalents, in\nrelation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nTo extend several public health law (PHL) provisions, and one social\nservices law (SSL) provision, set to expire in 2018.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill would extend section 5 of chapter 426 of the\nlaws of 1983, which relates to professional misconduct proceedings,\nuntil July 1, 2023.\n\nSection two of the bill would extend section 5 of chapter 582 of the\nlaws of 1984, which provides immunity from liability for members of any\nphysician committee acting without malice and within the scope of such\nmember's functions, until July 1, 2023.\n\nSection three of the bill would extend Section 60 of part B of chapter\n57 of the laws of 2015, which relates to energy audits and/or disaster\npreparedness reviews of residential health care facilities by the\nCommissioner of Health, until July 1, 2021.\n\nSection four of the bill would extend subdivision 12 of section 246 of\nchapter 81 of the laws of 1995, which relates to limited home care\nservices agencies, until June 30, 2019.\n\nSection five of the bill would extend subdivision 4-a of section 71 of\npart C of chapter 60 of the laws of 2014 amending the social services,\nwhich relates to fair hearings held in connection with appeals under the\nfully integrated duals advantage program, until January 1, 2021.\n\nSection six of the bill sets forth the bill's effective date, which is\neffective immediately.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill is necessary to ensure the continuation of specific laws of\ncritical importance to the Department of Health: PHL § 230 sets forth\nparameters governing the state board for professional medical conduct,\nPHL § 2808 relates to energy audits for residential health care facili-\nties, PHL § 3602 sets forth the definitions for Article 36, which\ngoverns home care services, PHL § 3605 relates to licensure of home care\nservices agencies, and SSL § 22(2-a) relates to fair hearings held in\nconnection with appeals under the fully integrated dual advantage demon-\nstration program.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nPHL § 230(10)(a), 11(c)(ii) and 11(g) were last extended in 2013, for an\nadditional five years.\n\nPHL § 230(11)(g)(v), 11(g)(vi), and PHL § 230(15) were last extended in\n2013, for an additional five years.\n\nPHL § 2808 was enacted in 2013 and has yet to be extended.\n\nPHL §§ 3602 and 3605 were last extended in 2017, for an additional year.\n\nSSL § 22(2-a) was last extended in 2017, for an additional year.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
