    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §5, Chap 426 of 1983; amd §5, Chap 582 of 1984; amd §230, Pub Health L; amd part B §60, Chap 57 of 2015; amd §246, Chap 81 of 1995; amd part C §71, Chap 60 of 2014",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8093A\n\nSPONSOR: HANNON\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 426 of the laws of 1983, amending the public\nhealth law relating to professional misconduct proceedings, in relation\nto the effectiveness of certain provisions thereof; to amend chapter 582\nof the laws of 1984, amending the public health law relating to regulat-\ning activities of physicians, in relation to the effectiveness of\ncertain provisions thereof; to amend the public health law, in relation\nto extending the demonstration period in certain physician committees;\nto amend chapter 57 of the laws of 2015, amending the social services\nlaw and other laws relating to energy audits and/or disaster prepared-\nness reviews of residential healthcare facilities by the commissioner,\nin relation to the effectiveness of certain provisions thereof; to amend\nchapter 81 of the laws of 1995, amending the public health law and other\nlaws relating to medical reimbursement and welfare reform, in relation\nto extending the effectiveness of certain provisions thereof; and to\namend chapter 60 of the laws of 2014, amending the social services law\nrelating to eliminating prescriber prevails for brand name drugs with\ngeneric equivalents, in relation to the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill would extend section 5 of chapter 426 of the\nlaws of 1983, which relates to professional misconduct proceedings,\nuntil July 1, 2023.\n\nSection two of the bill would extend section 5 of chapter 582 of the\nlaws of 1984, which provides immunity from liability for members of any\nphysician committee acting without malice and within the scope of such\nmember's functions, until July 1, 2023.\n\nSection three of the bill would amend PHL 230(11)(ii)(c) to extend the\ndemonstration period for the physician committee of the Medical Society\nof the State of New York or the New York State Osteopathic Society,\nwhose purpose is to confront and refer to treatment physicians who are\nthought to be suffering from alcoholism, drug abuse or mental illness,\nuntil July 1, 2023, in conformance with section two of this bill.\n\nSection four of the bill would extend Section 60 of part B of chapter 57\nof the laws of 2015, which relates to energy audits and/or disaster\npreparedness reviews of residential health care facilities by the\nCommissioner of Health, until July 1, 2021.\n\nSection five of the bill would extend subdivision 12 of section 246 of\nchapter 81 of the laws of 1995, which relates to limited home care\nservices agencies, until June 30, 2019.\n\nSection six of the bill would extend subdivision 4-a of section 71 of\npart C of chapter 60 of the laws of 2014 amending the social services,\nwhich relates to fair hearings held in connection with appeals under the\nfully integrated duals advantage program, until January 1. 2021.\n\nSection seven of the bill sets forth the bill's effective date, which is\neffective immediately, and specifies that sections one and two of the\nbill are deemed to have been in full force and effect on and after March\n31, 2018.\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill is necessary to ensure the continuation of specific laws of\ncritical importance to the Department of Health: PHL § 230 sets forth\nparameters governing the state board for professional medical conduct,\nPHL § 2808 relates to energy audits for residential health care facili-\nties, PHL § 3602 sets forth the definitions for Article 36, which\ngoverns home care services, PHL § 3605 relates to licensure of home care\nservices agencies, and SSL § 22(2-a) relates to fair hearings held in\nconnection with appeals under the fully integrated dual advantage demon-\nstration program. In addition, the bill pushes the expiration month and\nday to July 1st for the provisions of law, related to the state board of\nprofessional medical conduct found in sections one, two, and three of\nthe bill in order to avoid the expiration of those laws during the end\nof the state budget cycle.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nPHL § 230(10)(a) and 11(g) were last extended in 2013, for an additional\nfive years.\n\nPHL § 230(11)(ii)(c) was last extended in the 2018 Health and Mental\nHygiene Article VII budget bill (S.7507-C/A. A9507-C) for an additional\nfive years.\n\nPHL § 230(11)(g)(v), 11(g)(vi), and PHL § 230(15) were last extended in\n2013, for an additional five years.\n\nPHL § 2808(27) was enacted in 2013 and has yet to be extended.\n\nPHL §§ 3602 and 3605 were last extended in 2017, for an additional year.\n\nSSL § 22(2-a) was last extended in 2017, for an additional year.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately, with sections one and two of the bill deemed to have been\nin full force and effect on and after March 31, 2018.\n",
